      <article class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Here's a question I like to ask manufacturing teams: how long does it take to get one
          live machine value — a spindle temperature, a part count — onto a new dashboard? The
          honest answer is rarely “an afternoon”. It's usually weeks, a new interface, and one
          more thing to maintain.
        </p>
        <p>
          I split my time between a precision-manufacturing shopfloor and a university research
          group, and I see the same gap in both worlds. The algorithms are rarely the bottleneck.
          Getting the right data, with its context, at the moment it matters — that is.
        </p>
        <p>
          This article is the written companion to a talk I give on the <strong>Unified
          Namespace</strong> (UNS). The slides above are live: a small MQTT-style broker, a
          simulated factory and every demo run inside your browser. Click into them and use the
          arrow keys, or press <strong>Present</strong> to go full screen.
        </p>

        <h2>The problem is the shape, not the tools</h2>
        <p>
          Most factories are organised along the ISA-95 automation pyramid: sensors and machines
          at the bottom, then PLCs, SCADA, MES and ERP at the top. Each layer talks to its
          neighbour through its own interface. Data climbs one layer at a time, and every new
          question — a dashboard, a quality analysis, an AI use case — becomes a new integration
          project.
        </p>
        <p>
          The arithmetic is unforgiving. Connect systems point-to-point and <em>n</em> systems
          need up to <em>n</em>(<em>n</em>−1)/2 interfaces: 28 for eight systems, 66 for twelve.
          Worse, the next system you add costs <em>n</em> new interfaces. With a central hub,
          every system needs exactly one connection.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Drag the slider from 3 to 12 systems and switch between the two views on <a href="#3" data-slide="3">slide 3</a>.</p>

        <h2>What a Unified Namespace is</h2>
        <p>
          A Unified Namespace is a single, real-time hub where every system <strong>publishes
          what it knows</strong> and <strong>subscribes to what it needs</strong>, organised the
          way the business is organised. Four properties make it work:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A single source of current truth</strong> — the latest value of everything, in one place.</li>
          <li><strong>Event-driven</strong> — systems publish when something changes, not when someone polls.</li>
          <li><strong>Structured like the business</strong> — a hierarchy that people and machines can both navigate.</li>
          <li><strong>Open and lightweight</strong> — typically MQTT with simple payloads, so no single vendor owns your data flow.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A UNS is an architecture, not a product you buy. If one of these properties is missing
          you may still have built something useful — a message bus, a data lake — but not a UNS.
        </p>

        <h2>A topic is an address in your business</h2>
        <p>Every value in a UNS lives at a topic, and the topic reads like an address:</p>
        <pre><code>aimm-precision/eindhoven/machining/line-1/cnc-01/temperature</code></pre>
        <p>
          Enterprise, site, area, line, cell, data point — the ISA-95 levels. The path <em>is</em>
          the context: a value arriving on this topic already tells you which site, area, line and
          machine it belongs to. Anyone can guess where to find a value, and a program can
          subscribe to an entire line with one pattern. The message itself stays small:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{payloadExample}</code></pre>
        <p>
          In my experience, agreeing these levels and the naming convention is the most important
          design decision of the whole effort. It's governance disguised as a naming exercise.
        </p>
        <p class="try">
          Design your own namespace on <a href="#7" data-slide="7">slide 7</a>: type a company,
          sites and areas, choose a naming convention, and watch the tree build itself and start
          streaming values. You can download the structure as JSON as a starting point for your
          own discussion.
        </p>

        <h2>How the data moves</h2>
        <p>
          Underneath sits publish/subscribe messaging, usually MQTT. Machines, MES and quality
          applications publish to topics; dashboards, historians and agents subscribe. Publishers
          and subscribers never know about each other — only about topics. That's the decoupling
          that kills the spaghetti.
        </p>
        <p>Three details matter more than they first appear:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Retained messages.</strong> The broker keeps the last value per topic, so a dashboard that connects late immediately sees the current state.</li>
          <li><strong>Report by exception.</strong> Devices publish only on meaningful change. The generator demo shows the traffic you save compared with polling, live.</li>
          <li><strong>Payload format.</strong> Plain JSON is simple and flexible; Sparkplug B adds strict typing and birth/death certificates. My rule of thumb: start with JSON and a written contract, and move to Sparkplug B where you need its guarantees.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Seeing it work</h2>
        <p>
          Two demos make the argument better than any diagram. On
          <a href="#8" data-slide="8">slide 8</a> you subscribe with wildcards: <code>+</code>
          matches exactly one level, <code>#</code> matches everything below. One pattern gives you
          every machine's state across all sites; another gives you a single area. Invalid
          patterns are rejected exactly as a real broker would.
        </p>
        <p>
          On <a href="#9" data-slide="9">slide 9</a>, inject a fault on a machine. An andon board,
          an availability service and a maintenance agent all react — and none of them knows the
          machine exists, only the topics. The agent reads context from the namespace and proposes
          a work order, but a person approves it. Then plug in a brand-new energy monitor while
          everything is running. No producer is touched. That is the whole case for a UNS in one
          click.
        </p>

        <h2>For data engineers: it's the stream, not the store</h2>
        <p>
          The most common mistake I see is treating the broker as a database. A UNS holds the
          <em>current</em> state. History belongs in a historian or lakehouse, fed by a connector
          that subscribes like any other consumer. Backfills and model training read from storage,
          not from the broker.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things make a UNS a gift to data engineering. The topic path gives you dimension
          columns — site, area, line, machine — for free, without lookup joins. And if you treat
          each payload as a <strong>data contract</strong> (units, timestamps, quality flags),
          downstream pipelines stop breaking every time a gateway is updated. I go deeper into
          this in my companion deck on a
          <a href="/slides/data-platform/">data intelligence platform for manufacturing</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Where AI agents fit</h2>
        <p>
          Agents need two things from a factory: <strong>context in</strong> and <strong>governed
          actions out</strong>. A UNS provides both. Agents subscribe for context instead of
          needing a bespoke integration each, and they act by publishing to governed command
          topics rather than reaching into machines. Consequential actions wait for human
          approval, and because every action is itself an event, the whole loop is auditable.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24160166" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AIMM
          Framework for Manufacturing Intelligence</a> that I co-authored with Ivo Adan and Shane
          Ó Seasnáin, capabilities for human and agentic work are organised into four layers:
          cyber-physical, digitization, interaction and operator. The way I see it, the UNS is a
          core part of the digitization layer — the reusable factory information that both
          people and agents rely on.
        </p>

        <h2>What goes wrong</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Naming without governance.</strong> Agree the structure and who owns each branch before you scale.</li>
          <li><strong>Using it as a database.</strong> Keep history in a historian or lakehouse.</li>
          <li><strong>Payload chaos.</strong> Units, timestamps and quality flags need a contract from day one.</li>
          <li><strong>Security as an afterthought.</strong> TLS, authentication and per-topic access rules for who may publish and subscribe where.</li>
          <li><strong>Boiling the ocean.</strong> Start with one line and one use case; let the namespace grow with value.</li>
          <li><strong>Treating it as an IT project.</strong> Operators, IT and OT have to own it together, or it becomes another silo.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Three things to remember</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Integrate once, to the hub</strong> — not to each other.</li>
          <li><strong>Structure it like the business</strong>, so people and machines can find data.</li>
          <li><strong>Run it as a product</strong>: contracts, governance and history alongside.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If you're working on this — research, a pilot, a vendor evaluation — I'd like to hear
          what's working for you. You can find me on
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nitish-singh/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="note">
          About the demo: AIMM Precision is a fictional company, and every value in the slides is
          simulated in your browser. Nothing is sent anywhere, and the deck keeps working offline
          once the page has loaded.
        </p>
      </article>
